// Change: new rule
// Title: Transparent Tribe HTA Dropped In Public Core Folder
// Sigma ID: 528e397b-f1f2-4e8e-b360-9c9051f1ce26
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: @sohankanna
// Date: 2026-11-22 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218.005, G0134
// Tags: detection.emerging-threats
// Description: Detects the creation of an HTA file in "C:\Users\Public\core\", the working directory used by
//   Transparent Tribe (APT36), as reported by CYFIRMA in December 2025. In this campaign against
//   Indian government entities, a LNK disguised as a PDF ("Online JLPT Exam Dec 2025.pdf.lnk") runs
//   a remote HTA through mshta. When Kaspersky is installed, the loader creates
//   "C:\Users\Public\core\", writes a second HTA ("flow.hta") and runs it with mshta for
//   persistence.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://www.cyfirma.com/research/apt36-multi-stage-lnk-malware-campaign-targeting-indian-government-entities/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/0d96dbbaf239f29b10f0dea2f2a15c27dcbb3659/rules-emerging-threats/2025/TA/Transparent-Tribe/file_event_win_apt_transparent_tribe_public_core_hta.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where FolderPath contains ":\\Users\\Public\\core\\" and FolderPath endswith ".hta"
